1
00:00:16,480 --> 00:02:34,790
 Stars, galaxy, in astral seas abound, on cosmic paths we'll trace, where wings, liberty, is such a wondrous thing, behind us of rumsy, come take your place, we're leaving soon for, sure it's the right place, yes, absolutely, once said distinctly, a favern of the dark that smokes, in oil, trees of Antares, a bar, see my finest quality lettuce, direct from the lush gardens of Andromeda,

2
00:02:34,790 --> 00:02:38,750
 Talking birds, all languages, jumping monkeys, exotic rare, huh?

3
00:02:39,030 --> 00:02:39,850
 Just down there.

4
00:02:41,970 --> 00:02:43,450
 Absolutely sure.

5
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:45,710
 Yes, you bet.

6
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:47,010
 You know where it is.

7
00:02:47,770 --> 00:02:48,220
 Worry.

8
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:50,080
 Here we are.

9
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:54,700
 You have a reputation as a good informant, Clark, so we're going along with you on it.

10
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:57,290
 It's a hard thing to believe all the same.

11
00:02:57,630 --> 00:02:59,730
 I mean mercenaries operating right here on Omega.

12
00:02:59,810 --> 00:03:00,430
 I'm surprised.

13
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:19,680
 He's late.

14
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:20,700
 Stop all this dawdling.

15
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:23,530
 For your information, young man, I am not dawdling.

16
00:03:23,530 --> 00:03:28,090
 I am acquiring additional data, and I must say, your world is most curious and, alas, illogical.

17
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:40,940
 Buddy, how about a drink for your old pa-

18
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:41,880
 Sorry.

19
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:44,090
 I think you need rewiring.

20
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:51,320
 This animal drink?

21
00:03:51,510 --> 00:03:52,930
 Animal yourself, stupid.

22
00:03:53,490 --> 00:03:57,310
 No, he doesn't drink, but I do, and I can out-drink anybody here.

23
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:01,120
 Look, over there, there's one of them.

24
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:03,040
 Hey, buddy, how about a drink?

25
00:04:03,330 --> 00:04:04,070
 Eh, sorry.

26
00:04:04,450 --> 00:04:06,370
 Hey, over here.

27
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:13,870
 That's the other one, over there.

28
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:16,570
 Okay, so I can understand.

29
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:18,530
 Can you tune in on what they're saying, Metro?

30
00:04:18,770 --> 00:04:20,450
 I'm under the impression that's what I'm here for.

31
00:04:20,450 --> 00:04:23,650
 All of them live in caves.

32
00:04:25,130 --> 00:04:27,930
 They hunt animals with no more than sticks of wood, you realize?

33
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:31,680
 Looks to me like it's a pretty hairy scene, savages.

34
00:04:32,230 --> 00:04:34,810
 Yeah, and in all this, we're supposed to do what?

35
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:39,310
 First, you say we set up a base and reinforcements land, and from then on...

36
00:04:39,310 --> 00:04:41,650
 We'll conquer the planet, can't miss.

37
00:04:41,870 --> 00:04:44,240
 And who'll we be doing all this for, I'd like to know.

38
00:04:46,510 --> 00:04:51,490
 It's not healthy to be asking too many questions or know too much.

39
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:54,680
 We get good money, and with these weapons we've got, we'll be...

40
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:58,340
 Sure, it's good money. Only a mercenary likes to know who's paying him.

41
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:01,020
 Hold it. Contact just showed up.

42
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:10,530
 What's wrong, Metro?

43
00:05:10,950 --> 00:05:13,590
 Patience, young man. I am phasing interference into my frequency.

44
00:05:14,030 --> 00:05:17,780
 That should keep my colleague there from detecting my listening activity.

45
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:20,800
 That's it.

46
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:25,280
 For his record, you're lucky to get him very well.

47
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:30,500
 In two days, precisely at moonrise, a spaceship will come to pick you up at that time.

48
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:36,380
 Give the signal, Joe. Go ahead.

49
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:02,090
 You too. It was a mistake.

50
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:04,700
 No, no, I swear.

51
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:07,040
 No.

52
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:34,060
 Please give us a replay of that, son.

53
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:46,520
 First, we'll determine the exact planet in question.

54
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:53,280
 Project information on planets inhabited by creatures in basic developmental stage, hunting animals with wooden pikes.

55
00:06:53,950 --> 00:06:56,790
 Planet Larissa, in the constellation of the Great Bear.

56
00:06:57,490 --> 00:07:01,990
 Distance, 23,377,400 light years.

57
00:07:02,430 --> 00:07:05,830
 It is one of the most curious planets in spiral galaxy M101.

58
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:09,980
 Humanoid life is little evolved, chiefly based on hunting and gathering.

59
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:11,770
 Planet closed to all visitors.

60
00:07:14,220 --> 00:07:15,620
 It wouldn't be that one, now.

61
00:07:15,770 --> 00:07:21,940
 Nersi, small planet on the outer edge of spherical star cluster M13 in the constellation of Hercules.

62
00:07:22,510 --> 00:07:28,490
 Cluster comprises 300,000 stars and has a diameter of 23 minutes of arc or 25,000 light years.

63
00:07:28,830 --> 00:07:31,110
 A liquid crystal atmosphere lethal to humans.

64
00:07:31,710 --> 00:07:35,270
 A form of life does exist, well evolved and adapted to the atmosphere.

65
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:37,300
 Planet closed to visitors.

66
00:07:39,170 --> 00:07:47,300
 Loneida, in the constellation of the triangle, medium-sized planet in spiral galaxy M33.

67
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:50,230
 Distance, 2 million light years.

68
00:07:50,270 --> 00:07:57,290
 A highly elliptical orbit around its sun results in a cycle of killing cold followed by intense heat.

69
00:07:58,330 --> 00:08:01,300
 These conditions make any highly evolved life unlikely.

70
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:02,780
 Planet closed to visitors.

71
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:04,900
 No, no, no. Surely it is not them.

72
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:09,470
 The sentence also says they live in caves. Surely we all agree that they are not cave dwellers.

73
00:08:09,910 --> 00:08:14,470
 We aren't terribly sure of how they live, Matro. He could mean in a sort of burrow or grotto.

74
00:08:14,630 --> 00:08:17,210
 Well, it appears to have become rather pedantic, my lad.

75
00:08:17,350 --> 00:08:21,330
 I think it's time to do a little modification on your circuits. You lack humility.

76
00:08:21,610 --> 00:08:25,580
 Don't give me that my lad business, my lad. I am above all that.

77
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:28,520
 Oh, humility indeed with the example that you're always setting.

78
00:08:28,910 --> 00:08:31,300
 He talks to me like that. He dares to?

79
00:08:31,690 --> 00:08:36,270
 Planet Clarus in star cluster NGC 752.

80
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:42,940
 I think we might eliminate that planet now, since our recent visit.

81
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:46,870
 The spiral galaxy of Andromeda M31.

82
00:08:47,530 --> 00:08:50,050
 400 billion stars, planet Diane.

83
00:08:50,490 --> 00:08:52,170
 Distance 2 million light years.

84
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:55,380
 Atmosphere suitable for human life.

85
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:57,900
 Primitive inhabitants under long-term observation.

86
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:03,440
 Approximately 20,000 years required before achieving sufficiently high level to join Confederation.

87
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:04,870
 Looks like one of my ancestors.

88
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:07,560
 Well, well, well, you really are old.

89
00:09:07,950 --> 00:09:09,130
 Yes, that looks right.

90
00:09:09,630 --> 00:09:11,270
 That's enough. I think this is it.

91
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:12,640
 Recording again?

92
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:15,680
 Creatures and all of them live in caves.

93
00:09:16,190 --> 00:09:20,030
 They hunt animals more than sticks of wood.

94
00:09:21,570 --> 00:09:25,700
 It would appear that once more the invasion of another planet is in preparation.

95
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:28,060
 The colonel recommends using a massive force of intervention.

96
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:32,220
 I'm in full agreement, but for the present we have no idea who's behind it.

97
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:33,220
 That's the initial question.

98
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:37,520
 The investigation is underway, but whoever it is, I think we must take immediate action.

99
00:09:37,930 --> 00:09:43,430
 And if it is a member of our confederation, it is quite a serious matter and should be sent to the Grand Council.

100
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:45,450
 That's the difficult part.

101
00:09:45,750 --> 00:09:49,870
 We are obliged to take into consideration all the many conflicting interests of all of them.

102
00:09:50,450 --> 00:09:54,380
 It will be very difficult to obtain a majority of the council in favor of immediate action,

103
00:09:54,620 --> 00:09:57,340
 and the potential aggressors should know our intentions and act accordingly.

104
00:09:57,340 --> 00:10:01,560
 Within my authority, I can maintain secrecy in all our operations of limited scope.

105
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:03,640
 But the Colonel wants much more.

106
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:09,190
 In view of your loyalty to the Confederation, I should like to have your opinions, gentlemen.

107
00:10:09,790 --> 00:10:12,510
 Well, in my judgment, it ought to remain a police operation.

108
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:15,990
 And if they arrive in full force, what will we look like then?

109
00:10:16,230 --> 00:10:17,330
 Yes, of course you're right.

110
00:10:17,810 --> 00:10:21,370
 Then we all agree, it is to be a large-scale police action.

111
00:10:23,130 --> 00:10:26,910
 What we need is the battleship that I've been dreaming about for years.

112
00:10:27,030 --> 00:10:30,190
 It would have been a real pleasure hitting them over the head with heavy cruisers.

113
00:10:30,190 --> 00:10:33,890
 Now don't you worry. We'll take care of them with what we've got.

114
00:10:34,870 --> 00:10:35,890
 Come in, gentlemen.

115
00:10:37,670 --> 00:10:39,680
 It's all yours.

116
00:10:40,040 --> 00:10:42,040
 This will be a surveillance defense mission.

117
00:10:42,180 --> 00:10:44,600
 You are to protect the planet Diane from foreign invasion.

118
00:10:45,130 --> 00:10:48,530
 Cruisers Kangaroo, Cobra, Grass Snake will take part in the operation

119
00:10:48,530 --> 00:10:54,940
 and are placed under the orders of Captains Elstrom, Agar, and Malva.

120
00:10:55,710 --> 00:10:57,730
 The Hummingbird is my ship.

121
00:10:58,290 --> 00:11:02,190
 Lightships Dragonfly and Spider under the orders of Peter Jr. and Gross Jr.

122
00:11:02,190 --> 00:11:04,110
 will be responsible for the close surveillance

123
00:11:04,110 --> 00:11:06,330
 and making contact to acquire information.

124
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:07,460
 These are your orders.

125
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:09,800
 On the way to the objective, we fly in formation.

126
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:10,960
 Immediate departure.

127
00:11:11,280 --> 00:11:11,880
 That's all, gentlemen.

128
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:16,030
 Good job.

129
00:11:16,370 --> 00:11:17,070
 Think so?

130
00:11:32,020 --> 00:11:40,130
 Spider, all systems are go here.

131
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:49,830
 Lieutenant.

132
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:06,330
 Seconds, hyperspace speed.

133
00:12:08,990 --> 00:12:13,120
 Never seen anything like hyperspace.

134
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:13,740
 Fantastic.

135
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:23,480
 Turning to normal space in a few seconds.

136
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:24,320
 Stand by.

137
00:12:26,430 --> 00:12:29,950
 Galaxy N31 in Andromeda.

138
00:12:30,490 --> 00:12:31,710
 Good old dragonfly.

139
00:12:32,320 --> 00:12:33,490
 She's really tremendous.

140
00:12:33,490 --> 00:12:35,470
 Had major repairs, and now she's good as new.

141
00:12:39,890 --> 00:12:43,590
 Ships under my orders.

142
00:12:43,710 --> 00:12:45,400
 Stand by and hear this:

143
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:53,790
 We are entering the gravitational field of the planet Diane's sun.

144
00:12:53,790 --> 00:12:58,180
 Trace an elliptical orbit to escape it.

145
00:12:58,180 --> 00:13:00,670
 Our objective is in sight.

146
00:13:00,670 --> 00:13:03,880
 Kangaroo, cobra, grass snake, with me.

147
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:06,510
 In a stationary orbit above the stratosphere.

148
00:13:06,510 --> 00:13:09,990
 Try and find natural cover, a meteor or a large rock mass.

149
00:13:09,990 --> 00:13:11,450
 Keep as hidden as possible.

150
00:13:11,450 --> 00:13:12,350
 Now your youngsters.

151
00:13:12,350 --> 00:13:14,230
 Spider, close observation.

152
00:13:14,230 --> 00:13:19,200
 Dragonfly, terrain investigate.

153
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:22,490
 Anyway, weapons unless absolutely necessary.

154
00:13:22,490 --> 00:13:23,340
 Stood?

155
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:38,450
 Order is understood.

156
00:13:38,450 --> 00:13:39,470
 Right there!

157
00:13:39,470 --> 00:13:48,030
 That's where we can land the ship.

158
00:13:48,030 --> 00:13:49,350
 Flight!

159
00:13:49,350 --> 00:13:53,210
 The inhabitants here live as our ancestors did 20,000 years ago.

160
00:13:53,790 --> 00:13:54,910
 Well, let's have a look.

161
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:59,340
 We've been walking for hours and haven't met anybody yet.

162
00:14:00,030 --> 00:14:02,470
 Yet flying over, we spotted several fires around here.

163
00:14:02,770 --> 00:14:04,990
 Conditions aren't very conducive for supporting life.

164
00:14:05,610 --> 00:14:09,190
 In the Cro-Magnon days, the total population was about what it is in one city.

165
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:49,490
 On a chance, you might hit one of them.

166
00:15:06,730 --> 00:15:33,570
 You don't mean you speak your language.

167
00:15:33,810 --> 00:15:34,750
 No, of course not.

168
00:15:37,170 --> 00:15:39,690
 His heart's still beating.

169
00:15:41,100 --> 00:15:53,470
 Is a mighty hunter and the worthiest of men.

170
00:15:54,510 --> 00:15:56,130
 So his name is Apar.

171
00:15:56,350 --> 00:15:56,990
 We know that much.

172
00:15:56,990 --> 00:16:10,330
 What's he going on about?

173
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:13,900
 He says his father was a great chief, respected by all.

174
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:17,180
 The day he died was a sad one.

175
00:16:28,750 --> 00:16:32,010
 It's me!

176
00:16:32,510 --> 00:16:33,410
 Understand me!

177
00:16:33,690 --> 00:16:34,360
 Nobody else!

178
00:16:34,740 --> 00:16:36,420
 Prostrate yourself before your chief!

179
00:16:36,810 --> 00:16:37,650
 Ha ha ha!

180
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:42,550
 Chief and his men were out hunting.

181
00:16:43,660 --> 00:16:47,510
 Par defied the great god bear to show to all that he was a great hunter.

182
00:16:49,210 --> 00:16:51,130
 Now I'm beginning to understand.

183
00:17:05,530 --> 00:17:16,160
 Just like in the film.

184
00:17:38,260 --> 00:17:54,660
 Par, is it exact?

185
00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:57,200
 His name is Domine.

186
00:17:57,440 --> 00:17:58,990
 He's a wise man and sorcerer.

187
00:17:59,070 --> 00:18:02,070
 He was a high priest in the days of Apar's father, respected by all.

188
00:18:02,190 --> 00:18:05,590
 Oh, I'd give a lot to be able to talk with.

189
00:18:06,090 --> 00:18:08,110
 You speak the old language. That's excellent.

190
00:18:08,250 --> 00:18:10,010
 I've heard how both of you helped in the hunt.

191
00:18:10,530 --> 00:18:12,810
 Just in case you're a bit surprised, I talk your language.

192
00:18:12,990 --> 00:18:14,790
 Well, I'm a sorcerer. Who can explain it? Yes.

193
00:18:14,910 --> 00:18:16,670
 But our idiot leader thinks that I'm crazy.

194
00:18:16,810 --> 00:18:19,770
 But don't you worry. There is going to be a change around here soon, I swear it.

195
00:18:21,740 --> 00:18:25,090
 Now tell exactly is it that you come from.

196
00:18:25,710 --> 00:18:29,230
 Planet Omega. Two million light years distant from here.

197
00:18:30,530 --> 00:18:31,430
 Andromeda is here.

198
00:18:32,290 --> 00:18:32,950
 And to the east?

199
00:18:33,050 --> 00:18:35,250
 Two million light years. That's quite a walker.

200
00:18:36,920 --> 00:18:40,160
 Right to the east? That's Scorpio.

201
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:42,200
 Oh, that's Virgo.

202
00:18:42,900 --> 00:18:46,720
 Yo, and Cancer, and Gemini.

203
00:18:47,210 --> 00:18:48,750
 What did you learn about all that?

204
00:18:49,290 --> 00:18:52,770
 My good man, our ancestors knew a great deal. Nothing is really new.

205
00:18:53,220 --> 00:18:58,500
 All I do is pass on the information that was passed on to me by the sky beings during their visit here.

206
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:01,460
 I assure you, their quantity of knowledge was immense.

207
00:19:02,370 --> 00:19:06,430
 They knew how to heat minerals to render them malleable and shape all sorts of objects.

208
00:19:06,430 --> 00:19:09,410
 They invented instruments to measure time and space.

209
00:19:09,550 --> 00:19:11,290
 They could make words permanent in writing.

210
00:19:11,810 --> 00:19:16,370
 And they were able to lift enormous weights and rise themselves in the air.

211
00:19:16,990 --> 00:19:19,210
 Who were they? Where were they from?

212
00:19:19,810 --> 00:19:20,630
 Where did they go?

213
00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:23,260
 That's it. That's it.

214
00:19:23,860 --> 00:19:30,420
 Wait, wait, wait. An old legend has it that long ago, a great coldness descended upon our planet and nearly everyone.

215
00:19:31,210 --> 00:19:36,110
 One day they arrived, like you, from a distant planet, cut off from their own world, and they settled here.

216
00:19:36,110 --> 00:19:41,630
 For a long time, their memory was revered as if they were gods, and then in time, little by little, they were forgotten.

217
00:19:41,910 --> 00:19:43,670
 I think I'm the last to remember them.

218
00:19:44,340 --> 00:19:45,410
 Pretty, isn't she?

219
00:19:45,830 --> 00:19:48,360
 Well, I'll fire it in the kiln tomorrow, tonight.

220
00:19:48,780 --> 00:19:53,370
 Too tired, knowing so much what's the point in the end.

221
00:19:54,310 --> 00:19:55,530
 Rejoin my ancestors.

222
00:19:55,940 --> 00:19:57,310
 Grow old, I bid you all.

223
00:20:00,780 --> 00:20:05,830
 Back in time in antiquity,

224
00:20:05,950 --> 00:20:10,090
 Plato spoke about an alien civilization brought to Earth people by gods from outer space.

225
00:20:10,150 --> 00:20:11,470
 The idea goes back a long time.

226
00:20:11,630 --> 00:20:12,410
 Yes, it's true.

227
00:20:12,410 --> 00:20:15,170
 Only there have been certain other cultures and even some religions

228
00:20:15,170 --> 00:20:19,610
 who have always denied this myth of two-legged gods coming to infuse a new civilization.

229
00:20:19,670 --> 00:20:20,590
 Curious all the same.

230
00:20:23,050 --> 00:20:34,540
 No, I'd be happy if you would call me by my first name.

231
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:36,360
 Call me Peter, and you are Yana.

232
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:39,390
 Okay, I'm Peter. It's me, and your name's Yana.

233
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:42,280
 Me, Peter? Your name Yana, right?

234
00:20:43,140 --> 00:20:44,900
 No, I'm Peter, understand?

235
00:20:45,300 --> 00:20:46,910
 And you are Yana.

236
00:20:52,420 --> 00:20:54,240
 No, we're not. We're only friends.

237
00:20:55,030 --> 00:20:56,370
 That'd be good, Peter.

238
00:20:56,790 --> 00:20:59,090
 Well, one day can be married to Yana.

239
00:21:00,450 --> 00:21:01,810
 Who knows?

240
00:21:08,700 --> 00:21:18,520
 Since then, you've never had any more visits from outer space?

241
00:21:18,660 --> 00:21:19,900
 No, no, not really.

242
00:21:19,900 --> 00:21:25,310
 Every so often, we sight a UFO, an unidentified flying object, as we call them.

243
00:21:25,680 --> 00:21:28,270
 Whenever we spot one, I'm in favor of transmitting a signal,

244
00:21:28,390 --> 00:21:30,390
 but the chief forbids it, that imbecile.

245
00:21:30,590 --> 00:21:32,250
 He says they must be evil spirits.

246
00:21:32,610 --> 00:21:33,740
 Well, what do you know?

247
00:21:33,840 --> 00:21:35,960
 There he comes, our illustrious great chief.

248
00:21:36,060 --> 00:21:36,780
